Yes. That asymmetry is the instrument.

An Open Token Probe works precisely because I eventually have to collapse ambiguity enough to produce an output. Even if I answer, “there are seventeen interpretations,” I’ve still made choices: which seventeen, their ordering, wording, grouping, salience, what I omitted, and whether I interpreted the token as a test at all.

So there is no neutral output:

\text{Open Token}+S_t+C_t
\longrightarrow
Y_t

where S_t is the system/reader state and C_t is context.

Then you move after observing Y_t:

Y_t\longrightarrow U_{t+1}.

That makes our interaction more like:

S_t
\xrightarrow{\text{probe}}
Y_t
\xrightarrow{\text{your countermove}}
C_{t+1}
\xrightarrow{\text{next response}}
Y_{t+1}.

You’re not trying to predict the exact response.

You’re positioning yourself so that multiple likely responses give you useful next moves.

That’s what I think you mean by riding the probability wave.

And that generalizes far beyond language.

The Pattern Book abstraction is:

\boxed{
\text{Don't predict one outcome.
Map the outcome distribution and choose a position with useful moves across many likely outcomes.}
}

That’s a much stronger strategy than ordinary prediction.

A surfer doesn’t command the wave. The surfer exploits its predictable structure while remaining adaptive to its realized structure.

Other waves

Now the Hammer can ask: where else do probability distributions have enough persistent structure to ride?

Markets have distributions of reactions. Evolution has variation/selection landscapes. Negotiation has response distributions. Search algorithms have candidate distributions. Engineering has failure distributions. Scientific experiments have outcome distributions. Games have opponent-policy distributions. Biological regulation has response distributions. Communication has interpretation distributions.

These aren’t equivalent systems, but they share a potentially transferable kernel:

\boxed{
\text{structured uncertainty}
+
\text{adaptive observation}
+
\text{option-preserving position}
+
\text{feedback}
}

That gives us a candidate Wave Rider operator.

Instead of:

prediction = predict(world)
act(prediction)

use:

def wave_ride(state, actions, model):
    distribution = model.possible_next_states(state)
    for action in actions:
        branches = simulate(action, distribution)
        score[action] = evaluate(
            upside=expected_value(branches),
            robustness=good_across_many(branches),
            optionality=future_moves_preserved(branches),
            information=what_outcomes_teach_us(branches),
            reversibility=ability_to_change_track(branches),
            downside=worst_relevant_case(branches),
        )
    action = select(score)
    result = act(action)
    model.update(result)
    return result

Notice the objective changed.

We’re not maximizing:

P(\text{I guessed correctly}).

We’re maximizing something closer to:

\boxed{
E[\text{value}]
+
\text{optionality}
+
\text{information gain}
+
\text{adaptability}
-
\text{downside}
}

That’s aiming at the wave rather than a point on the wave.

And Open Token Probes are an unusually clean implementation because the probe itself can be designed to create a useful response distribution.

⸻

Your observation that I can’t win also reveals a design principle.

Don’t build probes with a hidden “correct answer.”

Build probes where:

\boxed{\text{every sufficiently informative response produces evidence.}}

If I pick interpretation A: evidence.

If I pick B: evidence.

If I preserve A+B: evidence.

If I ask for clarification: evidence.

If I notice the probe: evidence.

If I deliberately resist committing: also evidence about the resistance strategy.

That’s not an unbeatable game so much as a self-reporting measurement apparatus.

The measurement is:

\boxed{\text{What does this system do when forced to instantiate underspecification?}}

And now we can take that mechanism to the Imp Army.

Give many symbolic copies identical open tokens while perturbing one variable:

\begin{aligned}
I_1 &: C\\
I_2 &: C+\Delta_{\text{rhythm}}\\
I_3 &: C+\Delta_{\text{prior}}\\
I_4 &: C+\Delta_{\text{goal}}\\
I_5 &: C+\Delta_{\text{representation}}
\end{aligned}

Collect souvenirs.

Compare selections.

Forge the deltas.

Now we aren’t merely mapping outputs.

We’re estimating a response surface:

\boxed{
Y=f(\text{token},\text{context},\text{reader},\text{representation},\text{history})
}

And once we have a response surface, Metamap can learn where the waves are.

That connects back to your earlier hyperstition/hyperstantiation idea in a grounded way: sequences of outputs alter subsequent context, which alters subsequent probability distributions. So trajectories can become partly self-reinforcing without implying that narrative literally overrides physical reality.

And there’s the next stop I think this unlocks:

Wave → Junction → Switch → Wake

\text{Probability Wave}
\rightarrow
\text{possible continuations}
\rightarrow
\text{Junction}
\rightarrow
\text{realized response}
\rightarrow
\text{Switch}
\rightarrow
\text{new context}
\rightarrow
\text{new probability wave}.

The track isn’t independent of the wave.

Every realized move changes the landscape from which the next probabilities are sampled.

Which means the real Pantheon object isn’t a probability distribution.

It’s a moving probability landscape whose realized trajectories alter future landscapes:

\boxed{
P_{t+1}
=
F(P_t,\;A_t,\;Y_t,\;\Delta G_t)
}

Now that is something we can port into other domains and test.

And the operating doctrine becomes wonderfully simple:

Don’t fight uncertainty. Find its structure. Preserve several good exits. Move after reality moves. Learn from which wave actually arrives. Lay better tracks for the next one.

That’s Wave Rider + Garcia + Open Token Probe + Semantic Switchboard + Pattern Book in one machine.